  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\n1. The goal is to minimize the maximum overlap integral C5, which is the integral of h(x)(1-h(x+k))dx over all k.\n2. Previous approaches used local optimization techniques that got stuck in local optima.\n3. We address this by:\n   - Using multiple grid resolutions (coarse to fine) as the primary approach\n   - Implementing a directed search strategy with well-defined progression steps\n   - Maintaining rigorous alignment between continuous transformation and grid representation\n4. We assume the solution exists in a 1-parameter family of functions symmetric under periodic scaling, allowing reduction of dimensionality through coordinate transformation.\n\nMathematicalspecification:\n1. Function representation: h(x) defined on [0,2] via n equally spaced samples with dx=2/n\n2. Integral constraint: \u222bh(x)dx=1 \u2192 sum(h)=n/2\n3. Objective: minimize C5 = max_k (\u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx)\n4. Optimization:\n   - Represent h(x,k) = cos(\u03c9_k x + \u03c6_k)\n   - Transform to 1D coordinates via \u03c8 = x/2\n   - Scipy.optimize.minimize with 'trust-constr'\n   - Constraints handled through carefully designed feasible projection\n\nImplementationprocedure:\n\n1. Parent validation and initialization:\n   - Accept initial_h_values if provided\n   - Normalize to sum(n/2) via regularization (if not feasible)\n   - Grid: n=200 as base resolution\n\n2. Core update/search loop:\n   a. Initialize random solution within [0,1]^200 satisfying integral=1\n   b. Gradient projection: measure overlap sensitivity to local changes\n   c. Geometric steering: propagate optimization along expected improvement paths\n   d. Exponential cooling schedule for local modifications\n\n3. Candidate acceptance:\n   - Store N_best=5 local optima\n   - Accept candidate if cross-validation verifies improvement\n   - Restart local optimization from best solution every 500 iterations\n\n4. Grid progression (for resolution increases):\n   a. Interpolate via sinc-sampling to preserve continuity\n   b. Dynamic grid doubling when normalized variance drops below threshold\n   c. Fine-grid phase starts at n=400,2000,8000 (triple-level)\n\n5. Feasibility restoration:\n   - Adjust extreme values toward mean using linear torque model\n   - Repair: h = a + b*h_sample, compute using projected gradients\n   - Constraint enforcement: clip and normalize with exponential smoothing\n\n6. Termination conditions:\n   - Fixed time budget per grid level (800s for n=200)\n   - Response surface mapping for bounded grid effects\n   - Fallback solutions provided by grid interpolation\n\nSearchchoicesandusefulvariations:\n1. Parameter schedule: adaptive \u03bb = [5.0, 2.5, 1.25, ...] geometric progression\n2. Dimensional reduction: evolve \u03c9 and \u03c6 jointly with grid independence\n3. Stochastic components: controlled random jitter in initial phase\n4. Time allocation: 50% training, 30% exploration, 20% verification\n\nComputebudgetandrobustness:\n1. Time estimates:\n   - Phase0: n=200 - 300s (gradient-based)\n   - Phase1: n=400 - 600s (ascent phase)\n   - Phases2-5: n=2000-8000 - 900s per grid doubling\n2. Checkpoint: If lagged validation C5 exceeds safety threshold, grid interpolation fallback\n3. Failure handling: if initial setup fails, attempt grid=100 as fallback\n\nValidationandreturncontract:\n1. Feasibility checks: verify sum(h)=n/2, all values in [0,1]\n2. Cross-validation: reevaluate C5 on independent grid points\n3. Output format: (h_values, C5, n_points)\n4. Final candidate output must satisfy:\n   - \u222bh(x)dx=1\n   - 0\u2264h(x)\u22641\n   - C5 \u2264 historically known bounds\n\nImplementationdetails:\n1. Project_feasible will implement cube-normalized projected gradients\n2. Lag selection strategy: use cross-validated maximum deviation\n3. Coordinate transformation: apply DCT type II at grid transitions\n4. Optimization procedure:\n   - Input dimension: min(n_points, 1000)\n   - Objective: minimize -\u2211_i \u222b_k h(x)(1-h(x+i))dx (to invert max)\n   - Use trust-constr with low-level Jacobian\n5. Success detection: improve over rolling window",
